Again, I plead "HHEELLPP!!"

Has anyone installed POLYCENTER Networker Save and Restore v3.2 (aka
Legato Networker 4.2.3) on a dUnix platform, and been successful with
_any_ non-dUnix clients?

Details follow ...

I purchased POLYCENTER Networker Save and Restore v3.2, and the current
Unix client kit.  I have all the requisite licenses.  Everything _should_
work.  This is all based on Legato's Networker v4.2.3 products, but
Polycenter has mucked around with the server side of things.

I have the server software installed on an AXP 3000/400 running dUnix
3.2d.  I've installed the client software on an identical 3000/400, and it
works fine.  So, I have some assurance that the installation is OK.

Now, I have installed clients on two SGIs (Irix 5.3, and Irix 6.2), and a
Sun (Solaris 2.5).  I purchased the client kits from DEC.  And the SPD and
the release notes all claim that this is going to solve all my backup
problems.

These non-DEC clients _can_not_ save files to the DEC server.  They do
talk to the server, and some information is clearly interchanged.  But
they all fail with:

    "client <hostname> arch attribute does not match"

Looks like the arch attribute is wrong.  There is an arch box in each
client setup screen.  For the dUnix clients, it is filled in with
"decaxp."  For the SGI clients, I have tried the following: unknown, sgi,
irix, irix64, decaxp (hoping that the field described the server!), all in
lower and upper case.  Same message, always.  I haven't tried much with
the sun client, that comes next.

The arch field is not documented _anywhere_.  The message is originating
from the nsrd daemon on the server - it is the only executable that
contains this string (liberal use of grep supplied this information). 
There are no debug or verbose options for this daemon.  The arch field is
only visible in the client setup screen if the "details" option is turned
on.  An SGI server (that I can't back up to, but which I used to look at
the client screens) doesn't even have this field, so it looks like a
POLYCENTER addition.

Any help will be most appreciated.  Even a confirmation that you've gotten
non-dUnix clients, or SGI or Solaris clients to work.

DEC, are you listening?  Customer support says "you didn't order telephone
support?  Go away."  Thanks.  I bought $5000+ of software that is broken
out of the box, and no one will even talk to me?  I've been a strong
supporter of DEC hardware and software for a long time.  The DEC Fortran
team is a great example - helpful, supportive, etc.  The Networker Save
and Restore Unix Clients SPD says "Each client software subkit works in
conjunction with the NetWorker server product to provide the following
capabilities: Heterogeneous client support (SunOS, Solaris, HP-UX, IBM
AIX, SGI IRIS, etc.)"  The web pages make similar claims.  But it doesn't
work ...

