In <31F4F46C.2175@boromir.phcteam.com> Jim Drummey <jpd1@boromir.phcteam.com> writes:

>My company has some Alpha 2100s and 8200s running Digital Unix. The
>machines are equipped with HSZ40 controllers which have mirrored
>stripe sets configured. Those sets are passed up to the OS, which
>runs LSM and configures them as logical volumes. The logical volumes
>are then made part of AdvFs domains and filesets are built on those.
>My question is..isn't all this overhead counter productive for I/O?
>It looks as if LSM and AdvFs share some features so it would make
>sense to use on or the other, but not both, especially since the
>controllers are fanning out the I/O. I haven't seen anything from
>DEC outlining how these effect one another. Any help would be
>appreciated

I can't say what the I/O impact is since I didn't opt to use LSM on our 2100
w/HSZ40. I did use AdvFS. One are which I think that LSM might help is with
the setup. For example, when I was using dxadvfs, the AdvFS GUI thing, most
of my RAID sets on the HSZ40 didn't show up as available disks, so I had to
do the majority of my setup using the command line. Once the domains and
filesets were created by hand, they did show up in the GUI. I'll bet that
when you used LSM, you didn't have that problem, right?

