In <31F55628.63DE@iisc.com> Charles M Richmond <cmr@iisc.com> writes:

>You are of course correct. The one is arguably an error in that
>the compiler follows K&R by default instead of the current 
>ansi standards.  The other is a bonehead oversight on my part.

Traditionally, cc is the K&R front end of the C compiler on a Unix system.
If you need to invoke the compiler in ANSI mode, just use the c89 front
end.

This is particularly handy when dealing with multiple platforms, because
it avoids you the need of remembering (or figuring out) the magic cc
incantation required to invoke the C compiler in ANSI mode on each
platform.  c89 is a standard POSIX.2 feature, hence it is supported by
many vendors, including HP, DEC and IBM.

