From: alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com (Alan Rollow - Dr. File System's Home for Wayward Inodes.)
Subject: Re: Why use LSM with AdvFS?
Date: 1996/07/25
Message-ID: <4t91ik$6vf@nntpd2.cxo.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 170555618
distribution: world
references: <31F4F46C.2175@boromir.phcteam.com>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com (Alan Rollow - Dr. File System's Home for Wayward Inodes.)
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1



In article <31F4F46C.2175@boromir.phcteam.com>, Jim Drummey <jpd1@boromir.phcteam.com> writes:

>My company has some Alpha 2100s and 8200s running Digital Unix. The
>machines are equipped with HSZ40 controllers which have mirrored
>stripe sets configured. Those sets are passed up to the OS, which
>runs LSM and configures them as logical volumes. The logical volumes
>are then made part of AdvFs domains and filesets are built on those.
>My question is..isn't all this overhead counter productive for I/O?

It depends on how you're using LSM and whether you really need
that feature in this configuration.  Among the features provided
are:

o  Volume striping.  The advantage that host based striping has
   over controller striping is that can you stripe devices across
   multiple controllers and I/O adapters.

o  Volume mirroring.  The advantage that host based mirroring has
   over controller based mirroring is that you can mirror devices
   across multiple controllers and I/O adapters.  While a dual-
   redundant HSZ40 can provide a lot of availability it is still
   connected to the host by a single SCSI adapter and cable.  If
   that cable or controller breaks, you can't get to your data
   anymore.

o  Volume concatentation.  If you wanted to concatenate all the logical
   units presented by a group of HSZ40s into a single large device you
   could use LSM for this.  I'm not sure you'd want a single volume
   that big but you do it.

o  Volume partitioning.  While you can disklabel the device presented
   by an HSZ40 the partition table in the label limits you to 7
   partitions (8 if you're careful).  If you need more, then using
   LSM to carve out small volume from a large chunk of space is
   probably the way to go.

o  Tracing and logging - A rather obscure feature that problems
   mounds of data, but which could be useful if you want to
   collect I/O traces.  See the voltrace manual page for more
   information.

o  Interaction with ASE.  ASE failover may rely on application
   disks being in a disk group, but I'm not sure.

>It looks as if LSM and AdvFs share some features so it would make
>sense to use on or the other, but not both, especially since the
>controllers are fanning out the I/O.

AdvFS only does file level striping and no mirroring.  While file
level striping is useful, often it is better to have volume level
striping.  Of course the HSZ40 can provide this, but then you're
limited to the bandwidth of a single FWD SCSI adapter or the
through put of a single HSZ40 (for one device).

LSM is an extra layer of the kernel to go through for an I/O
request and arbitrarily limits I/Os to 64 KB.  It is also
extra set of management commands to be learned, the GUI can
do most things and is pretty easy to use.

If you only have a single HSZ40 (or redundant pair) and are
satisfied by the performance and availability, then you probably
don't need LSM for striping or mirroring.  If you have multiple
controllers and adapters, then you can mix the HSZ40 RAID features
and LSM to get better performance or availability:

1.  Better availability.  Create the stripe sets on each HSZ40,
    but then mirror them with LSM so that one controller/adapter/
    cable set doesn't become a single point of failure.

2.  Better performance.  Take advantage of the mirroring on the
    HSZ40 (and automatic replacement from the SPARESET), but
    stripe the mirrored devices across multiple I/O adapters.

Keeping everything under LSM may allow extending the size of a
volume when the software using can cope with volumes that grow
(unfortunately, neither AdvFS nor UFS do).  For some types of
volumes you can mirror them at any time, if you have the spare
space.

>Jim Drummey      jpd@drum.terranet.com
