D. White (dswhite@email.unc.edu) wrote:
[...]
 > Is there any way that I can be given access to the
 > /usr/sbin/reboot command for my own machine, without giving me
 > full root access?  I don't need any other superuser functions
 > besides "reboot", and both the sys.admin and I would be happier
 > if I could do it myself rather than hunt her down each time the
 > computer crashes.  She thinks it's possible to give me reboot
 > control without other root privileges, but isn't sure how.  Can
 > anyone suggest a way?

You could create a pseudo-user "reboot" with the user id "0"
(root), home directory "/" and shell "/usr/sbin/reboot". Be sure
to chose a good password.

If the user "reboot" logs in, the command /usr/sbin/reboot will
be executed automatically.

Some systems require all login shells to be listed in a file,
i.e. "/etc/shells". Your sysadmin will have to add
"/usr/sbin/reboot" to this file.

Heiner
