> #include <stdio.h>

> main()
> {
>         unsigned long E1 = ~0 ;
>         printf("%lu %lu %d %d\n",E1 >> 1, E1/2,0x20,'\x20');
>         return 0;
> }
> 
> Found what I think are 2 bugs in the standard Digital UNIX
> cc compiler this week.
>
> Not sure of the OS version, but I think it is 3.2 patch
> level  
>
> The first one is that >> on a full unsigned  long of
> 1 bits fails to do anything. 

> The second is that the compiler does not recognise hex
> chars.  

The default mode for C compilers on Digital UNIX is -std0, commonly
referred to as common or K&R mode.  K&R C did not support hex escape
sequences.  If you recompile in either of the ANSI modes, -std or 
-std1, you'll find escape sequences are supported and you get the
results you expect.

Concerning your first problem, I ran your program and the output 
did do the shift.  Can you post the exact version of the OS and
show the output you're getting?

Thanks,
Joe Mario



