From: pdchapin@unix.amherst.edu (PAUL D. CHAPIN)
Subject: dUnix 4.0 and auth.db
Date: 1996/07/23
Message-ID: <4t2q6c$lnj@tom.amherst.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 170263562
organization: Amherst College, Amherst MA, USA
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.sys.dec


Does anybody have any experience running dUnix 4.0 with the auth.db in
place in an environment with a very high login rate.  I'm concerned
about the possibility that the auth.db file will create a bottleneck
in the same way that the protected password database in NIS did where
every log on would tie up the file.  I have a lot of low impact users
but at certain times of the day, usually around dinner time, I get
swamped with people jumping on and off checking their email.  When we
tried NIS last year we had a lot of time out problems - so severe that
we had to abandon NIS.

Also, has anybody had any problems not converting to auth.db.  I
haven't done that so far and haven't had any problems - at least none
that I can trace to the lack of auth.db as a source.  While auth.db
may potentially improve performance, my gut reaction is if it ain't
broken, don't fix it.  Login performance hasn't been a major issue.
However, DEC has told me that conversion wasn't an option but was
mandatory.

DEC is suppose to be looking in to this for me, but I would be
interested in any field experience people have had.


--


Paul Chapin
Unix Systems Manager
Amherst College




