In article <4svets$ig5@si-nic.hrz.uni-siegen.de>, unger@lewis.informatik.uni-siegen.de (Thomas Unger) writes:

|> When compiling some X-sources the executable sometimes
|> produces some strange effects like swapped x/y axis
|> when using the mouse. (e.g. fvwm2) 
|> 
|> As far as I understand this is because of a change
|> in the X event structure. I 'solved' this by using
|> consistent Xlibs and Xincludes. When I switched
|> to DU4.0 i expected this to obsolete, but it's still
|> the same.

Most customers perfer binary compatability over change for the
sake of change.

The X libraries and headers that are shipped with Digital Unix 
have a number of small but important differences when compared
with the X Consortiums sample implementation. These differences
are only noticable if you try to mix & match Digitals supported
libraries with ones you are building yourself.

These differences are unlikely to be removed at any time in the
future because the costs associated with binary incompatability
is just too great to justify making the change.

If you want to build your own libraries, then use the versioning
that is supported by the X11R6 sample implementation so that your
libraries are distinct from the Digital supplied libraries. This
will of course increase the memory usage of your system if you are
using both sets at the same time..... 



