If the application's running on the UNIX system and displaying on the PC,
then, in XWindows terms, the PC is running an X Display Server and the
Client Application is on the UNIX system.

I've not heard of XWIN32, but have had no major problems running applications
from Digital UNIX displaying to PC's running Digital's eXcursion product, nor
have I heard of major problems with other X display servers such as
Hummingbird's Exceed.  Most "general problems" folks have mentioned stem from
the fact that PC's network interface cards just are not built for the same
sort of performance that UNIX systems can deliver, and are easily overrun
causing retransmission delays.  The other common complaint, getting less 
frequent these days, is with X applications that were written with 
workstations (with huge monitors with 1280x1024 resolution, for example) in 
mind that don't fit on a 'normal' PC screen (with 640x480 - 1024x768 
resolution) or with applications which "require" one or more X extensions, 
which are not guaranteed to be in all display servers.

