From: roman@portal.stwing.upenn.edu (Roman Gollent)
Subject: Digital Unix 4.0 Feedback / FYI
Date: 1996/07/17
Message-ID: <4sjiau$ta2@netnews.upenn.edu>
X-Deja-AN: 169378081
organization: University of Pennsylvania
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


	Here is a list of problems / bugs / suggestions that we have
come up with in the past couple of days, while testing out Digital
Unix 4.0.  I have split the list into two parts, OS bugs and User
interface design issues.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Jon Boone:		tex@isc.upenn.edu
Roman Gollent:		roman@isc.upenn.edu
Mark R. Levinson:	mrl@isc.upenn.edu
Mark Sirota:		msirota@isc.upenn.edu

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OS bugs:

1) tail -r /dev/null dumps core (/dev/null is used in this example
because it is an easily reproduced example). 
-- Mark Levinson

2) If a directory has a file in it named the same as the
directory, and you move the directory to itself, the file is removed.
	% mkdir a
        % touch a/a
        % ls -la a
        total 16
        drwxr-xr-x   2 msirota  system      8192 Jul 17 12:47 .
        drwxrwxrwt   4 root     system      8192 Jul 17 12:47 ..
        -rw-r--r--   1 msirota  system         0 Jul 17 12:47 a
        % mv a a
        mv: rename a to a/a: Invalid argument
        % ls -la a
        total 16
        drwxr-xr-x   2 msirota  system      8192 Jul 17 12:47 .
        drwxrwxrwt   4 root     system      8192 Jul 17 12:47 ..
        %

This happens under DU3.0, 3.2C, 3.2D, and 4.0, but not under
any other operating system I've been able to get my hands on.
-- Mark Sirota

3) A symlink from /dev/fddrive --> /dev/fd0c is expected by mtools but
not present. 
-- Roman Gollent

4) prot.h expects acl.h in /usr/include.  acl.h is actually located in
/usr/sys/include and there are no symlinks from /usr/include pointing
to it.
-- Roman Gollent

5) Changing the ethernet card (tu0) from multi-cast to non-multicast,
or vice versa, causes non-local ICMP packets to be emitted with source
address 0.0.0.0. 
-- Jon Boone

6) Pattern matching on ps t is broken.  ps tp[a-e] will work fine.
If the pfilter devices are installed, ps tpf will report:
	ps: /dev/pf: not a terminal

ps ought to, at the very least, check for a /dev/ttypf before
checking for a /dev/pf, although it would probably be best if it
weren't checking for /dev/p* at all. 
-- Mark Levinson

7) Once networking is configured, CDE will cease to function properly
if the network interface (tu0) is taken down while one tries to load
an app or perform a CDE function (like logging in/out on console).  I
don't know if this is supposed to happen with the way CDE was
designed, but I consider this a serious bug (Failsafe login or XDM is
an option, however). 
-- Roman Gollent

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Interface issues:

1) You can't set nfs IO daemons to 0 on the nfs config app (Even
though 0 is specified as a valid number by the interface). 
-- Roman Gollent

2) Also in the bind utility: If you have one name server in the
configuration and you need to delete it, you can't do so until after
you add another nameserver. 
-- Roman Gollent

3) The dtadvfs utility is very inflexible when it comes to specifying
file systems flags (eg: I can't specify that I want to mount a
filesystem rw,nosuid,nodev).  You may want to change this interface
to include some default entries, plus a user adjustable field to add
additional options.  Another alternative would be to simply add
buttons that can be clicked on and off, each specifying a certain
characteristic (ie: rw, ro, userquota, groupquota, nosuid, nodev,
etc.). 
-- Roman Gollent 

4) The Bind GUI does not allow for proper configuration of the
"search" directive.  We use "search" on our machines to specify
both the dccs.upenn.edu and upenn.edu domains. 
-- Roman Gollent

5) The doconfig script ought to allow me to specify options 3-7 (The
way the installation script does) instead of 3 4 5 6 7.  This is
merely a user interface consistency issue, but probably worth fixing.
-- Jon Boone & Roman Gollent

6) There are several people (who are also fond of human factors) in
our department who feel that "Installing n of m subsets" ought to be
"Installing subset n of m" (Where n is the number of the current
subset and m is the total number of subsets to be installed).
-- Everybody

7) I can't really isolate what causes this problem, but the icons in
the various application manager menus will not appear upon opening of
a menu, submenu.  
-- Roman Gollent

8) The screensaver utility will not show all of the screen saver
options the first time a user starts into CDE (It only shows "Swarm of
Bees", when it should be showing a lot more).  Logging out and logging
back in fixes this behavior. 
-- Roman Gollent
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

There is one more issue, which is related to Digital Unix 4.0, but
actually an issue that needs to be dealt with by the Free Software
foundation.  GNU tar-1.11.8, make-3.74 and diffutils-2.7 all fail to
compile due to undefined preprocessing macros contained in GNU's
fnmatch.h file.  The offending ifdef block looks similar to this (It
varies slightly between the different packages):

#if !defined (_POSIX_C_SOURCE) || _POSIX_C_SOURCE < 2 || defined (_GNU_SOURCE)
#define FNM_FILE_NAME   FNM_PATHNAME /* Preferred GNU name.  */
#define FNM_LEADING_DIR (1 << 3) /* Ignore `/...' after a match.  */
#define FNM_CASEFOLD    (1 << 4) /* Compare without regard to case. */
#endif

My solution was to move the missing definitions outside of this block.
This produced normally behaving binaries, as far as I can tell.

As it has been stated by someone else on the newsgroup, gcc does not
compile under 4.0.  We haven't seriously looked into this problem
because DEC cc and DEC cxx both managed to compile most of the apps
that we have tried so far.  We would welcome any other hints / tricks
/ FYIs that people may have. 

Regards,
Roman Gollent

--
