Hi Gerard,

Gerard_van_Houwelingen (ctlgvh@euronet.nl) wrote:
> 
> How do I create an advanced file system. one week ago they installed 4 x 2Gb 
> wide scsi disks in a Alpha 2100 but installed them as normal unix file system. 
> my question is : how do I set them up as a advanced unix filesystem, if 
> possible. No data is stored yet on these disks

This is very easy. Assume that your disks names are, say,  /dev/rz8c, 
/dev/rz9c, /dev/rz10c and /dev/rz11c. Do this:

1.) Unmount the disks if they are currently mounted.
2.) # mkfdmn /dev/rz8c my_domain
    # mkfset my_domain my_fs
    # addvol /dev/rz9c my_domain
    # addvol /dev/rz10c my_domain
    # addvol /dev/rz11c my_domain
    # mkdir /big
    # mount -t advfs my_domain#my_fs /big

Replace my_domain and my_fs with any names you like.
The /big filesystem now has 8GB free storage.

see "man advfs" for a list of other related advfs commands.

Thats all. Be sure to have AdvFS support compiled into the kernel, though,
and the AdvFS Utilities installed.

Hope that helps -- Tom

