In <4sj84c$oni@hacgate2.hac.com> jd@ahi.es.hac.com (Jeff Dykzeul) writes:

| I tried

| 	# restore -tvf /dev/nrmt1h
| 	# restore -tvf /dev/nrmt1h

Unix tape drivers leave the tape positioned where it was last sitting
when yu close the non-rewind tape (or rewind completely with the rewinding
version).  "restore t" just reads the directories, all of which are near
the front of the dump file - the effect is that after the first restore
the tape is positioned in the middle of the forst dump, a later restore
won't make much sense of that.

Try...

restore -tvf /dev/nrmt1h
mt -f /dev/nrmt1h fsf
restore -tvf /dev/nrmt1h

(restore also has a file skipping option you could try, but that's
generally useful only when you have the tape rewound and want to access
the N'th tape file).

kre



