Richard Abraham  <abrahad@govonca3.gov.on.ca> wrote:
}I have a new Alpha Server 2100 5/250 and am dissappointed to find that
}it only supports 7 scsi devices.  It has 3 internal slots, an internal
}storage works rack and an external scsi port.  According to the
}documentation if I use the internal storage works rack I can't use the
}external port.  Since it has a CD-ROM drive installed I am left with
}only 6 slots for my 8 storage works drives.  I was also hoping to
}connect our TZ867 tape drive to the back of it.

    There are 8 SCSI addresses, 0 through 7 -- the CPU is 7 leaving
    0..6 for your devices.

}The documentation in this area which came with the hardware is poor so I
}am confused.  It says the bus can be configured in single bus mode or
}split bus mode.  What is the advantage of one over the other?  Is their
}any way I can use the external port and the storage works rack?

    Even if you could put 8 devices on one SCSI bus, you probably
    wouldn't want to for performance reasons.  Typically, you
    would purchase a second SCSI controller card and do something
    like this:

             +----[internal]---[internal]---[internal]
             |                                  |
        controller-0                       ------------ storage works box
                                           |    0     |
                                           |    1     |
                                           |    2     |
                                           |    3     |--[term]
                                                        <- remove jumper
        controller-1 ----------------------|    0     | between 2 halves
                                           |    1     |
                                           |    2     |---[ext connector]
                                           ------------          |
                                                            [tape drive]
                                                                 |
                                                            [terminator]

John
