nntp-posting-user: grunwald




The normal configuration for the 2100 has a single SCSI chain.

The biggest problem with the 2100 (the older model, not the 2100A)
is the limited number of PCI slots and the limited support for EISA
cards.

If you have a spare PCI slot, you can buy a PCI-SCSI controller for
abour $90 (e.g., ASA computers online catalog). The Alpha supports the
NCR 54C810 chipset (narrow, fast SCSI). I think you can also use the 54C815
(which should be fast & wide). You can get generic versions of these PCI
cards for $70-150 -- there's not a whole lot on the card other than the
chip, so most should work.

Alternatively, DEC will sell you a PB2HA-SA, an EISA-based 1-port
narrow fast-SCSI controller, probably for ~$300-400. DEC also has a
variety of PCI based SCSI options.

On the 2100, the storage works shelves need 2 SCSI controllers (or a
RAID controller) to populate an entire shelf. You can actually put 2
shelves in the systems.

The 2100 was the first PCI device Digital made, so I can see the
compromises they made, but it would be nice if they had added many
more PCI slots.



