Bob Vickers wrote:
> 
> Processes are not being killed. They are trying to allocate or write virtual
> memory and failing because it is not there. Probably they are getting bus
> errors or memory faults.

Processes will be killed and unfortunately the victim is chosen somewhat
arbitarily (whoever is being swapped out at the time swap space is
exhausted).  Choosing the "best" process to kill is not very easy and 
generally not the one with the most virtual memory allocated.

If you want to continue to run in lazy swap mode, perhaps you could
experiment with different amounts of swap space until the problem
goes away.  This should give you the minimal amount of swap space
needed for the workload running on the system.

Pat

