>>>>> "Bob" == Bob Vickers <R.Vickers@dcs.rhbnc.ac.uk> wrote:
 Bob>   It is almost certainly directly related to your swap mode. In
 Bob> immediate swap mode an error occurs at malloc time if swap space
 Bob> is unavailable, and a well written process can easily recover
 Bob> from this. In deferred mode the malloc succeeds but random
 Bob> errors occur later on when the process tries to write to the
 Bob> memory. This is the penalty you pay for the disc space saving
 Bob> which deferred mode gives you.

>>>>> "Peter" == Peter da Silva <peter@nmti.com> writes:
 Peter> That's pretty broken. It should be possible to simply allocate
 Peter> memory pretty close to real memory + swap without problems,
 Peter> and then have malloc (actually, sbrk()) and friends generate
 Peter> errors at that point. I don't see why it should ever be
 Peter> necessary to kill random processes.

Errors can only be reported by sbrk() when swap-space allocation is 
being done early. The whole point of deferred swap allocation is *not*
to allocate swap space simply because sbrk() has been called; this
allows, for example, the use of sparse-memory algorithms (without
requiring gigabytes of swap). The early allocation scheme has some
particular disadvantages; on most systems I've encountered it was always
necessary to configure substantially more swap that could ever actually
be used, simply to ensure that memory *could* be allocated for things
that, in fact, were never going to consume swap space.

With deferred allocation, swap space is reserved when pages are actually
referenced. Since there is no obvious mechanism for the kernel to report
an out of memory condition on what is, after all, an innocent memory
access by the program, there is little choice except to kill the process
(or some other process, to free up some swap space).

I'm not familiar with Digital Unix (last time I used it it was called
Ultrix :-), but I have had the usual amount of fun with deferred swap
allocation on AIX. AIX has a particular order of preference for which
processes it kills when running low on swap, and also has the ability
to set early allocation on a per-process basis, with these processes
being exempt from paging-space kills.

The whole immediate vs deferred allocation thing is a tradeoff, anyway.
Having it optional seems to me to be a reasonable behaviour. I am less
impressed by the apparent majority of implementations that appear to
have simply rejected deferred allocation out of hand.

-- 
Andrew Gierth (andrewg@microlise.co.uk)



