I thinks that ssh maybe could provide what you need. Amongst others
ssh provides secure (there are different encryption schemes such as
DES and IDEA available) rsh and rcp equivalents. I addition ssh
includes tools to create secure ports for use with any application.

Another nice feature with ssh when used as a rsh/rlogin-replacement is
that it automatically encrypts X11 connections. To accomplish this it
checks wether $DISPLAY is set on the local side. On the remote side
the sshd daemon creates a dummy X-server and sets $DISPLAY to point to
that server. X requests are then relayed through the ssh connection
and are displayed by the local ssh client. No more xauth or xhost
hassles. Beware: Dont't use programs like PhotoShop (or any
application that pumps large amounts of data) if you don't have a
heavy CPU or ypu will end up with a computer that does nothing but
encrypting that megapixel photo of Pamela Anderson (or what else you
might prefer).

Check out http://www.cs.hut.fi/ssh for more info.












