>After this, the only way to go on is to press CTRL C.
>Then the boot process continues and at the end the xdm comes up
>and shows the X Windows login srceen.

You can go to single-user, by first going to the monitor level (>>> prompt) 
and then setting the boot level with "set bootos_flags s". Doing a "set 
auto_action halt" is also a good idea (this will make the machine stop at 
monitor level when resetting.

Then boot with command "boot", and mount disks with "bcheckrc". The startup 
scripts are located in /sbin/rc0.d, rc2.d and rc3.d. The startup scripts 
starts with "S". Execute the manually to see what is the cause for your 
problems.

good luck.

