Subject: Re: "mmeserver" and "nsrd" - can we disable their startup?
Date: 1996/07/01
Message-ID: <4r9kml$poq@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 163178496
references: <836208398.4521.0@fersys.demon.co.uk>
organization: University Communications, Inc.
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


In article <836208398.4521.0@fersys.demon.co.uk>,
 <mlb@fersys.demon.co.uk> wrote:
>We have a OSF/1 V3.2C system, in which there are /sbin/rc3.d scripts to start
>two processes: "mmeserver" and "nsrd".  As far as I know we don't need them
>(system is going to customer site running dedicated application software),
>so can we just disable their startup?   (Avoids growing logfiles etc).

Basically, yes, we do this to most of our machines. They're just symbolic
links to the actual script, I usually create a directory "disabled" and
move them there so I know what order they were in. You can also move the
kill links from rc1.d as well, but it will only produce a few random
messages, if anything, when you shut down.

Some of the scripts can be shut down using the values in rc.config; use
the various setup configure scripts to set most of them, but check the
startup scripts themselves to see if there are any other config values
it uses.

If you're using static routing and want to set the initial time on boot
from an NTP server and that server is not on your local net, you should
move the settime script to come after route (or, alternatively, move
route to be right after inet, so the rest of the log files actually
have the correct times in them!). This appears to have been fixed,
sort of, in 4.0 (by moving settime to be after route, not by moving
route to be before settime, so if the system comes up with the time
totally wrong, syslog and several others will be started with an
incorrect initial time).

Note that if you rename or remove script links, an update installation
will resurrect the old ones, and won't delete any renamed or new ones.
You're probably best off restoring them to their original form before
doing the update, then fix them again afterwards.

I wish more of the startup scripts had rcmgr config options to disable
them. We do NOT want sendmail running at all on our system, for
instance.



