>manager. Seems system did not start up dtwm window manager (my console
>is a dumb terminal) so I don't see the workspace manager. Of course I
>lost all my nice mwm stuff when I kill the NCD window manager and type
>/usr/dt/bin/dtwm
>
> Looks like a long weekend of reading. So much for Canada Day!

Don't go out of your way to use CDE; its too buggy and bloated to
worry about.  I left it installed long enough to find that the file
manager will lock me out of my home directory when removing execute
permissions on a file contained therein.  Just delete all the CDE junk,
save ~60MB of disk space, and continue on with a stable
{ct,t,m,vt,tvt,fv}wm environment. As an added bonus, most of the buggy
GUI sysadmin tools won't work without CDE installed.

On the plus side, I'm *VERY* glad that Digital UNIX 4.0 has retained
the pristine startup procedures that that it had before GUI sysadmin
tools were shipped with the base OS.  I was afraid that after the GUI
tools became available, the startup scripts might become an
unintelligible mess not readable by humans (like in HP-UX 9.x, one of
the first OSes to ship with GUI sysadmin tools).  Vi is still just
about the only sysadmin tool you really need in DU 4.0.

Another huge plus is the HTML (and text!) documentation.  Now you can
actually print out something of interest, or (even better) easily
search for something with grep.  No more need to limp along with
bookreader!

The only serious problem I'm having with 4.0 is pseudo terminal
handling.  Some commercial programs we have that ran fine under
(BSD-style) ttys in 2.0, 3.0, 3.2, 3.2A and 3.2C don't work under 4.0
& exit with error messages such as:

cled: can't open pty: No such device or address

However, they work fine when run as root & ULTRIX versions work when
translated via DECMigrate. I've spent a while attempting to build a
kernel that used only the old BSD-style ttys, but I've had no joy.
Has anybody out there managed to avoid /dev/pts/* in 4.0?  What kernel
options did you use?  Is it even possible?

Thanks,

Drew

