In article <4r87ji$blm@gwdu19.gwdg.de>,
Juergen Holm <holm@Theorie.Physik.UNI-Goettingen.DE> wrote:
>WEILL Philippe (philippe.weill@aero.jussieu.fr) wrote:

<snip>
>> using mount option size=8192 wsize=8192 is better
>> Linux Client READ from OSF1 Server : ~400Ko/s
>> Linux Client Write to  OSF1 Server : ~150Ko/s

<snip>

>But the problem is, that i don't find this option in the OSF exports man-page.
>The default setting for the OSF server seems to be snyc-write. But, there MUST
>be a way set async writes! Please mail me if you solve this problem.

Using asynchronous writes under NFS version 2 is very dangerous &
strongly discouraged.  The best solution would be to upgrade the
version of NFS on the client to NFS Version 3.  The NFSv3 spec allows
for safe asynchronous writes (for a good explanation of NFSv3, see the
NFSv3 paper in the 1994 Summer USENIX conference proceedings).

If Linux really doesn't do NFSv3, there are other operating systems
available for Intel platforms that do (since you're running Linux, I'm
assuming the client is a PC).  Solaris x86 version 2.5 is one, and
NetBSD-current is another. 

Drew

