We do our backups for the UNIX-machines using the tar-command
and the path-name the automount-daemon gives the mounted machine
(usually something like /mount/hostname or /tmp_mnt/hostname).
All you need is the automount-daemon running and an entry allowing
you root-access to the exported partitions (off course this 
may cause security-problems !!!!!).


For the Win 3.11 machines we use SAMBA (a public domain software
making your UNIX-box act like an NT-Server), which works very nice
(we kicked out any PC-NFS). For information see

http://lake.canberra.edu.au/pub/samba/samba.html

I dont know if this wirks with WIN95 too, but i think so.

If you think of using SAMBA you can do backups like that
(this is inserted from another Mail concernig this subject)

We've been doing the same thing here.  We have a few Win95/WFW/WinNT
machines
that we're backing up.  The machine we use for the backups is running
OSF1 with
smbclient.  For our machine there is a /dev/rmt0h and /dev/nrmt0h.  Both
are
the tape device.  However, the first tells the tape to rewind after
writing to
it; the second does not.  I couldn't get a tar c /dev/nrmt0h to work,
but a tar
cf /dev/nrmt0h works fine.  I assume it's just treating the tape like a
file
and dumping data out there like a file.  When it reads in, it treats it
like a
file also.  As it turns out a execute tars to /dev/nrmt0h and when I go
to look
at the files I have to do this:

tar tf /dev/rmt0h    (to rewind the tape)
tar tf /dev/nrmt0h   (contains first tar file)
tar tf /dev/nrmt0h   (contains nothing)
tar tf /dev/nrmt0h   (contains second tar file)
tar tf /dev/nrmt0h   (contains nothing)
tar tf /dev/nrmt0h   (contains third tar file)
tar tf /dev/nrmt0h   (contains nothing)
                .
                .
                .

Anyway, it works and I have been able to restore files.  Just takes a
little
while to step down through the preceding tar files and the "blanks".

(end of insert)



