Paulo Melo wrote:
> 
> Hello,
> 
> My school has recently bought an Alphaserver 1000 4/266, with Digital
> Unix V3.2 41. The problem I'm having with it is that I can only seem to
> be able to use the built-in graphics board to show 640x480 in X
> windows... I don't really have much documentation (other than
> what bookreader could tell me) and so although I tried to RTFM, I
> could not find what I wanted to read.
> 
The information you require is in the release notes, which is both in
the Bookreader files and on the Digital Unix installation CDROM.

> sizer tells me it uses a Cirrus graphics board with 640x480
> resolution. The boot messages tell me the same thing. But I believe
> it has exactly the same board that a twin I have running NT that can
> show 800x600 (x256). So:
> 
> a) Am I right believing its something to do with the device support on
>    the kernel? Is there some device driver (or loadable module) I could
>    use to get better resolution?
> 
No, the problem (and the solution) is nothing to do with device drivers.
It is simply a matter of configuring the X server. Quoting from the
release notes :-

modify: /usr/var/X11/xdm/Xservers
and change the last line to contain the following:
:0 local /usr/bin/X11/X -nice -2 -screen 800

apply the modification by issuing the following commands:

# /sbin/init.d/xdm stop
# /sbin/init.d/xdm start

(or simply reboot the machine - my suggestion, not in release notes).

> b) X cant really be used with 640x480. Is there any way to have a virtual
>    screen size (like XFree does) of say 1024x768 even if the display
>    size is only 640x480, with the X server that comes with the machine?
No, unfortunately the standard X server in Digital Unix does not support
virtual screens.

> 
> Best regards,
>         Paulo Melo
> 
> 
Hope this helps.

Dave B.

P.S. the above fix can also be used for other, higher resolutions, if
your hardware supports it. However if you are using the On-board
controller (i.e. you don't have a separate graphics controller card
installed) 800 x 600 is the highest resolution available.



