On Wed, 03 Jul 96 23:36:28 GMT, David Herdman
<dherdman@osil.demon.co.uk> wrote:
>I have been using an old AXP PC150 at the office with UTP just fine.
>I brought it home and plugged it into my BNC network and it won't
>play. Lots of ln0 carrier lost messages. Now I know on some machines
>you have to set a console variable to switch between BNC & UTP but 
>this is DE422 ethernet card and the console won't let me issue
>an ERA .. command for it.
>
>Anyone give me a clue?
>

Dave,

the answer is a hardware one. On the DE422 the port choice is made via
the large blue (usually) "shunt" connector. Take a look at the
ethernet card and you'll see a large blue plastic 2-piece that jumpers
two rows of a three row set on pins. Yours is set for UTP. Remove the
shunt connector, spin it 180 degrees and place it over the other two
rows of pins. Power up and things should be fine ....


Grant Tilby



