In article <lally-0507961420140001@maxine.mst.lanl.gov>,
Bryan Lally <lally@lanl.gov> wrote:
>The other part of the problem is, if I turn on the ether interface, things
>that route off my subnet seem to be using the ether.  NFS, telnet, etc. 
>This is _not_ what I want, as I have FDDI connectivity to machines on
>several other subnets.  But the default route (as reported by netstat -r)
>is going out ln0.  Anyway I can set the default route to go out the fddi
>port?

Did you try editing /etc/routes and changing the default router to
whatever is appropriate for the FDDI net?

Take a look at /etc/rc.config - that has all the information that the
/sbin/init.d/inet (and the link in rc3.d) uses.  You can tweak the
NETDEV_* and IFCONFIG_* entries to not turn on your ethernet interface
at all, or turn it on after you have turned on your FDDI interface.
You can also run /usr/sbin/netsetup to change things around.

Patch 197 to CAP makes it support Digital FDDI interfaces.  I don't
have any systems that are using CAP and FDDI, so I can't say for sure
if the patches work.


